<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Yemen Vilayet</h1><p class="st-meta">1849 CE – 1918 CE · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second Ottoman province of Yemen, held against constant Zaydi revolts until the empire's defeat in 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> returned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> in 1849, occupying the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodeidah">Hodeidah</a>. After the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> increased the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea's</a> value, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-muhtar-pasha">Ahmed Muhtar Pasha</a> led an expedition that took Sanaa in 1872, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qasimid-state">Qasimid State</a>'s last remnant and creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, schools and courts and drew a border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">British Aden Protectorate</a> in 1904-1914. Taxation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> law provoked repeated Zaydi risings: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yahya-hamid-al-din">al-Mansur Muhammad</a> and from 1904 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya</a> besieged Sanaa, and Ottoman losses were heavy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-daan">Treaty of Da'an</a> in 1911 gave Yahya authority over the Zaydi highlands under Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Yahya stayed loyal during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> of 1918 the Ottoman garrisons withdrew and he took Sanaa, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a>; the coast and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a> followed different paths.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yemen-vilayet.jpg" alt="Yemen Vilayet" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: User:Orwellianist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_(1844%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1849 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmed Muhtar Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (Zaydi and Sunni)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Yemen</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ottoman Yemen (1849-1918), Vilayet of Yemen</dd></dl></aside></div>
